2.121.m:
“I will kill you here with this very sword, of this there is no doubt!”
Seeing him approach her own place, she uttered a roar of ‘huṃ’;
at that sound of ‘huṃ’, the vile Dānava fell,

2.124.m:
of this sacred Purāṇa named the Padma in the Kali age, O best of men. / In the Kali age, sinful men must go to the ocean of hell;

why would they listen to that sacred work, the accomplishment of the four aims of life? / By whom this sacred Purāṇa named the Padma is heard,

by him the entire achievement of the four aims of life is accomplished. / Therefore, sacrifices such as the horse sacrifice are lost, O noble-minded one;

in the Kali age they have gone to heaven together with the Vedas, their limbs, and their accents. / Whoever, endowed with goodness, faithful, devoted to the Lord,

a righteous man, desires to listen together with his son and his wife, / great faith first arises in him for the sake of listening;

for a man who listens, no great obstacle can intrude. / But unfaithfulness first arises for the man who recites,

and greed arises for the listener, O best of the twice-born. / Sent by Lord Viṣṇu, a dreadful, great delusion

caused his ruin, as he listened to it constantly. / Daily there arise revilers, mockers, and sinners.

It should be known by the wise one: 'An obstacle of mine has now arisen / and is seen, O Vyāsa!' Then he should perform a fire offering

Through great good fortune the five sections are obtained, O earth-gods;
heard, they bestow liberation—truth, truth, there is no doubt.

This second [section], the Bhūmikhaṇḍa, is completed.

